// Interview preparation bank + mock interview bank (4 tracks x 4 sets, difficulty increases by set).
// Each question: [question, model answer, keywords ("a|b" = synonyms), difficulty]

const PREP = {
  dsa: [
    ['What is the difference between an array and a linked list?', 'Arrays store elements contiguously, giving O(1) index access but O(n) insertion/deletion in the middle; their size is fixed (or amortised for dynamic arrays). Linked lists store nodes with pointers, giving O(1) insertion/deletion at a known node but O(n) access, plus extra memory for pointers and poorer cache locality.', ['contiguous', 'pointer|node', 'o(1)', 'o(n)', 'access', 'insert|insertion'], 'easy'],
    ['How does a hash map achieve O(1) average lookup?', 'A hash function converts the key into a bucket index in an array, so lookup goes directly to that bucket. Collisions are handled by chaining or open addressing. With a good hash function and a load factor kept low by resizing, buckets stay small, giving average O(1); the worst case is O(n).', ['hash function', 'bucket|index', 'collision', 'chaining|open addressing|probing', 'load factor|resize|resizing'], 'medium'],
    ['When would you use BFS instead of DFS?', 'Use BFS to find the shortest path in an unweighted graph or to process nodes level by level, because it explores in increasing distance order using a queue. DFS uses a stack/recursion and suits cycle detection, topological sorting, connected components and exhaustive path search.', ['shortest path', 'queue', 'level', 'stack|recursion', 'cycle|topological'], 'medium'],
    ['Explain the difference between dynamic programming and greedy algorithms.', 'Both need optimal substructure. Greedy makes one locally optimal choice per step and never revisits it, which is correct only when the greedy-choice property holds. DP considers all choices for each subproblem and stores results to avoid recomputation (memoisation or tabulation), so it handles overlapping subproblems such as 0/1 knapsack.', ['optimal substructure', 'local|locally', 'overlapping', 'memo|memoization|memoisation|tabulation', 'knapsack|example'], 'hard'],
    ['How would you detect a cycle in a linked list?', 'Use Floyd\'s fast and slow pointers: slow moves one step and fast moves two. If there is a cycle they eventually meet; if fast reaches null there is no cycle. This takes O(n) time and O(1) space. To find the cycle start, reset one pointer to head and move both one step until they meet.', ['fast', 'slow', 'meet', 'o(1)', 'null'], 'medium'],
    ['What is the time complexity of quicksort and why can it degrade?', 'Quicksort averages O(n log n) because good pivots split the array roughly in half. With poor pivots — for example always choosing the first element on already-sorted input — partitions are unbalanced and it degrades to O(n²). Random or median-of-three pivots make the worst case unlikely.', ['o(n log n)', 'pivot', 'o(n^2)|o(n²)|quadratic', 'sorted', 'random|median'], 'medium'],
    ['How does a heap differ from a binary search tree?', 'A heap only guarantees that each parent is smaller (min-heap) or larger (max-heap) than its children, so it gives O(1) access to the minimum/maximum and O(log n) insert/extract. A BST orders all left descendants below and right descendants above each node, supporting ordered traversal and search in O(h). Heaps are usually stored in arrays as complete trees.', ['parent', 'minimum|maximum|min|max', 'o(log n)', 'search|ordered|inorder', 'array|complete'], 'hard'],
  ],
  oops: [
    ['Explain the four pillars of OOP.', 'Encapsulation bundles data with methods and restricts direct access (private fields with getters/setters). Abstraction exposes only essential behaviour through abstract classes or interfaces. Inheritance lets a class reuse and extend another (is-a). Polymorphism lets one interface have many implementations, through overloading (compile time) and overriding (runtime).', ['encapsulation', 'abstraction', 'inheritance', 'polymorphism', 'example|for example|interface'], 'easy'],
    ['What is the difference between an abstract class and an interface?', 'An abstract class can have state, constructors, and both abstract and concrete methods, and a class can extend only one. An interface defines a contract; a class can implement many interfaces. Since Java 8 interfaces can have default and static methods, but no instance state. Use an abstract class for shared base behaviour and interfaces for capabilities.', ['constructor|state', 'multiple|many', 'contract', 'default', 'extend|implement'], 'medium'],
    ['What is method overriding and how is it different from overloading?', 'Overriding is a subclass providing its own implementation of a parent method with the same signature; the version is chosen at runtime based on the object type. Overloading is defining multiple methods with the same name but different parameters in the same class, resolved at compile time.', ['subclass|child', 'same signature|same name', 'runtime', 'compile time', 'parameters'], 'easy'],
    ['Explain composition vs inheritance with an example.', 'Inheritance models an is-a relationship (Car is a Vehicle) and tightly couples child to parent. Composition models has-a (Car has an Engine) by holding references to other objects, so behaviour can change by swapping components. Prefer composition for flexibility and to avoid deep hierarchies.', ['is-a', 'has-a', 'coupling|coupled', 'flexible|flexibility', 'example|car|engine'], 'medium'],
    ['What are the SOLID principles?', 'Single Responsibility: one reason to change per class. Open/Closed: open for extension, closed for modification. Liskov Substitution: subclasses must be usable in place of parents. Interface Segregation: many small interfaces over one large one. Dependency Inversion: depend on abstractions, not concrete classes.', ['single responsibility', 'open', 'liskov', 'interface segregation', 'dependency inversion'], 'hard'],
  ],
  dbms: [
    ['What are ACID properties?', 'Atomicity means a transaction completes fully or not at all. Consistency means it moves the database between valid states respecting constraints. Isolation means concurrent transactions do not see each other\'s partial results. Durability means committed changes survive crashes, typically through write-ahead logs.', ['atomicity', 'consistency', 'isolation', 'durability', 'transaction'], 'easy'],
    ['What is normalisation? Explain 1NF, 2NF and 3NF.', 'Normalisation organises tables to reduce redundancy and update anomalies. 1NF requires atomic values with no repeating groups. 2NF requires 1NF and no partial dependency of non-key attributes on part of a composite key. 3NF requires 2NF and no transitive dependency, so non-key attributes depend only on the key.', ['redundancy', 'atomic', 'partial dependency', 'transitive', 'anomal'], 'medium'],
    ['What is an index and what are its trade-offs?', 'An index is an auxiliary data structure, usually a B+ tree, that lets the database find rows without scanning the whole table, speeding up searches, joins and ORDER BY. The trade-offs are extra storage and slower INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE because indexes must be maintained. Index columns that are frequently filtered or joined.', ['b+ tree|b-tree|tree', 'faster|speed', 'storage|space', 'insert|update|write', 'scan'], 'medium'],
    ['Explain the different transaction isolation levels.', 'Read Uncommitted allows dirty reads. Read Committed prevents dirty reads but allows non-repeatable reads. Repeatable Read keeps rows read in a transaction stable but may allow phantom rows. Serializable makes transactions behave as if executed one after another. Higher isolation means more correctness but less concurrency.', ['read uncommitted', 'read committed', 'repeatable read', 'serializable', 'dirty|phantom'], 'hard'],
  ],
  sql: [
    ['Write a query to find the second highest salary.', 'SELECT MAX(salary) FROM employees WHERE salary < (SELECT MAX(salary) FROM employees); — or with window functions: SELECT salary FROM (SELECT salary, DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC) r FROM employees) t WHERE r = 2. DENSE_RANK handles duplicate top salaries correctly.', ['max', 'subquery|select', 'dense_rank|limit|offset', 'where'], 'easy'],
    ['What is the difference between INNER JOIN and LEFT JOIN?', 'INNER JOIN returns only rows that have matches in both tables. LEFT JOIN returns all rows from the left table plus matching rows from the right; when there is no match, the right side columns are NULL. LEFT JOIN is used to find unmatched rows, for example customers without orders.', ['inner join', 'left join', 'match', 'null', 'all rows'], 'easy'],
    ['What is the difference between WHERE and HAVING?', 'WHERE filters rows before grouping and cannot use aggregate functions. HAVING filters groups after GROUP BY and is used with aggregates like COUNT or SUM, e.g. departments with more than 10 employees.', ['before', 'after', 'group by', 'aggregate|count|sum', 'filter'], 'easy'],
    ['Explain window functions with an example.', 'Window functions compute a value across a set of rows related to the current row without collapsing them into groups. Example: SELECT name, dept, salary, RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY dept ORDER BY salary DESC) FROM employees ranks employees within each department. Others include ROW_NUMBER, LAG, LEAD and running SUM.', ['over', 'partition by', 'rank|row_number', 'without|not collapse|each row', 'lag|lead|running'], 'hard'],
  ],
  os: [
    ['What is the difference between a process and a thread?', 'A process is an independent program in execution with its own address space and resources. Threads are units of execution inside a process that share its memory and resources, so they are cheaper to create and switch between but need synchronisation to avoid race conditions.', ['address space|memory', 'share|shared', 'lightweight|cheaper', 'context switch|switch', 'synchronization|synchronisation|race'], 'easy'],
    ['What is a deadlock? What are the necessary conditions?', 'A deadlock is a state where a set of processes wait forever for resources held by each other. The four Coffman conditions are mutual exclusion, hold and wait, no preemption and circular wait. Breaking any one — for example ordering resource acquisition to prevent circular wait — prevents deadlock.', ['mutual exclusion', 'hold and wait', 'no preemption', 'circular wait', 'prevent|avoid'], 'medium'],
    ['Explain paging and virtual memory.', 'Virtual memory gives each process a large logical address space. Paging divides it into fixed-size pages mapped to physical frames through a page table; the TLB caches recent translations. Pages not in RAM are loaded on a page fault from disk, and a replacement policy like LRU chooses which page to evict.', ['page table', 'frame', 'page fault', 'tlb', 'lru|replacement'], 'medium'],
    ['Compare FCFS, SJF and Round Robin scheduling.', 'FCFS runs processes in arrival order; it is simple but short jobs can wait behind long ones (convoy effect). SJF picks the shortest burst, minimising average waiting time, but needs burst estimates and can starve long jobs. Round Robin gives each process a time quantum in turn, giving good response time for interactive systems; quantum size affects overhead.', ['fcfs|first come', 'sjf|shortest job', 'round robin', 'quantum', 'waiting time|starvation|convoy'], 'hard'],
  ],
  cn: [
    ['What is the difference between TCP and UDP?', 'TCP is connection-oriented, reliable and ordered, using a three-way handshake, acknowledgements, retransmission, flow control and congestion control. UDP is connectionless with no delivery guarantee or ordering, so it has lower overhead and latency — used for DNS, video streaming and games.', ['connection', 'reliable', 'handshake', 'order|ordered', 'latency|faster|streaming'], 'easy'],
    ['What happens when you type a URL in the browser?', 'The browser checks caches, then resolves the domain via DNS to an IP address. It opens a TCP connection and performs a TLS handshake for HTTPS, sends an HTTP request, and the server returns a response. The browser parses HTML, requests CSS/JS/images, builds the DOM and renders the page.', ['dns', 'ip', 'tcp', 'tls|https', 'http request|request', 'render'], 'medium'],
    ['Explain the OSI model layers.', 'Physical (bits on the medium), Data Link (frames, MAC addresses), Network (packets, IP routing), Transport (end-to-end delivery, TCP/UDP), Session (managing sessions), Presentation (encoding, encryption), Application (HTTP, DNS, SMTP). TCP/IP merges these into four layers.', ['physical', 'data link', 'network', 'transport', 'application'], 'medium'],
    ['What is the difference between HTTP and HTTPS, and how does TLS work at a high level?', 'HTTPS is HTTP over TLS. During the TLS handshake the server presents a certificate signed by a trusted CA, the client verifies it, and both agree on session keys using asymmetric cryptography. After that, data is encrypted with fast symmetric encryption, ensuring confidentiality and integrity.', ['tls|ssl', 'certificate', 'handshake', 'asymmetric|public key', 'symmetric|encrypt'], 'hard'],
  ],
  se: [
    ['Explain the SDLC.', 'The Software Development Life Cycle is requirements gathering, analysis, design, implementation, testing, deployment and maintenance. Models like Waterfall do these sequentially, while Agile repeats them in short iterations with continuous feedback.', ['requirement', 'design', 'testing|test', 'deployment|deploy', 'maintenance'], 'easy'],
    ['What is Agile and how does Scrum work?', 'Agile is an iterative approach that delivers working software in small increments and adapts to feedback. Scrum organises work in sprints of 1-4 weeks with roles (Product Owner, Scrum Master, Development Team) and events (sprint planning, daily stand-up, sprint review, retrospective). Work is tracked in a product backlog.', ['iterative|iteration', 'sprint', 'product owner', 'stand-up|standup', 'retrospective|review|backlog'], 'medium'],
    ['What are unit, integration and system testing?', 'Unit tests check individual functions or classes in isolation, often with mocks. Integration tests check that modules work together, e.g. API with database. System tests check the complete application against requirements. Acceptance testing then confirms it meets user needs.', ['unit', 'integration', 'system', 'isolation|mock', 'acceptance'], 'medium'],
  ],
  'system-design': [
    ['How would you design a URL shortener?', 'Requirements: create short links and redirect quickly at high read volume. Generate a unique id (auto-increment or distributed id generator) and encode it in base62. Store code → URL in a database, cache popular codes in memory, run stateless app servers behind a load balancer, and respond with HTTP 301/302 redirects. Add analytics asynchronously.', ['base62|hash', 'database', 'cache', 'load balancer', 'redirect|301|302'], 'medium'],
    ['What is the difference between horizontal and vertical scaling?', 'Vertical scaling adds CPU/RAM to one machine; it is simple but has hardware limits and a single point of failure. Horizontal scaling adds more machines behind a load balancer; it scales further and improves availability but needs stateless services or data partitioning.', ['vertical', 'horizontal', 'machine|server', 'load balancer', 'limit|single point'], 'easy'],
    ['Where would you use caching, and what are the risks?', 'Cache frequently read, rarely changed data — sessions, product pages, computed results — in memory to cut latency and database load. Risks are stale data and invalidation complexity; strategies include TTLs, write-through or cache-aside, and eviction policies like LRU.', ['latency', 'stale|invalidation', 'ttl', 'lru|eviction', 'cache-aside|write-through'], 'hard'],
  ],
  project: [
    ['Explain your final-year project.', 'Use problem → solution → tech stack → your role → challenge → result. Example: "BrainBridge solves fragmented placement preparation. I built the Express REST API and PostgreSQL schema. The hardest part was streak calculation across time zones, which I solved by storing the student\'s local date with a unique constraint. Practice time rose 57% in our pilot."', ['problem', 'tech|stack|database|react|java', 'role|i built|i designed', 'challenge', 'result|impact'], 'easy'],
    ['What would you improve in your project if you had more time?', 'Show self-awareness with concrete, technical improvements: add automated tests and CI, improve performance with caching or indexing, strengthen security (rate limiting, input validation), and gather user feedback for UX improvements. Explain why each matters.', ['test|testing', 'performance|cache|index', 'security', 'feedback|users', 'because|why'], 'medium'],
    ['How did you handle a disagreement in your project team?', 'Use STAR: Situation (two members disagreed on the database), Task (choose quickly), Action (listed requirements, built a small prototype, compared objectively), Result (chose PostgreSQL due to relational data; team agreed and delivered on time).', ['situation', 'disagree|disagreement|conflict', 'listened|discussed|compared', 'result|agreed', 'team'], 'medium'],
  ],
  hr: [
    ['Tell me about yourself.', 'Present → Past → Future in 60-90 seconds: current status and key skills, 1-2 strongest achievements or projects with results, and why this role is your next step. Avoid family details and reading out your resume.', ['student|graduate|engineer', 'project', 'skill', 'internship|experience|achievement', 'role|opportunity'], 'easy'],
    ['What are your strengths and weaknesses?', 'Give a job-relevant strength backed by an example, and a genuine but non-critical weakness with concrete steps you are taking to improve. Avoid clichés like "I am a perfectionist" without evidence.', ['strength', 'weakness', 'example', 'improve|working on', 'result|feedback'], 'easy'],
    ['Where do you see yourself in five years?', 'Show growth aligned with the company: deeper technical expertise, more ownership, perhaps mentoring juniors, while contributing to impactful products. Avoid mentioning plans to leave soon.', ['grow|growth', 'skill|expertise', 'responsibility|ownership|lead', 'company|organisation|team', 'learn'], 'easy'],
    ['Describe a time you worked in a team.', 'Use STAR: the team context, your specific responsibility, actions like coordinating tasks or helping a teammate, and the measurable result. Credit others while making your role clear.', ['team', 'role|responsibility', 'action|i', 'result', 'communicat|coordinat'], 'medium'],
    ['How do you handle conflict at work?', 'Stay calm, understand the other perspective, focus on facts and the shared goal, propose options, and escalate respectfully if needed. Back it with a short real example and outcome.', ['listen|understand', 'calm|respect', 'facts|data', 'goal', 'example|result'], 'medium'],
    ['Why do you want to join our company?', 'Mention specific research: products, technology, culture or values, and connect them to your skills and career goals. Generic answers ("big brand, good salary") are weak.', ['product', 'culture|values', 'technology|tech', 'skill', 'goal|grow|learn'], 'medium'],
    ['Are you willing to relocate?', 'Answer honestly and positively. If flexible: "Yes, I am open to relocating — I see it as a chance to learn and adapt to new teams." If you have constraints, state them politely and mention preferred locations.', ['yes|open|willing|flexible', 'learn|opportunity|adapt', 'location|city'], 'easy'],
    ['What are your salary expectations?', 'For freshers, research the market and the role, and express flexibility: "I understand the standard package for freshers in this role is in a certain range; I am comfortable with your company\'s standard offer and more focused on learning and growth." Avoid quoting a number without research.', ['research|market', 'standard|range|package', 'flexible|comfortable|open', 'growth|learning'], 'medium'],
  ],
  etiquette: [
    ['How should you dress for an interview?', 'Dress one level more formal than the company norm: formal or smart-casual, clean, ironed and well fitted, with neat grooming and minimal accessories. For video interviews, dress fully and choose plain colours.', ['formal', 'clean|neat|ironed', 'grooming', 'video|camera'], 'easy'],
    ['What should you do if you don\'t know the answer to a question?', 'Stay calm, be honest, explain what you do know about related concepts, describe how you would approach or find the answer, and offer to reason it out. Never bluff.', ['honest', 'calm', 'related|know', 'approach|reason|learn'], 'easy'],
    ['What questions should you ask at the end of an interview?', 'Ask thoughtful questions about the role and team: what a typical day looks like, how success is measured in the first six months, the tech stack, learning and mentoring opportunities. Avoid asking only about salary or leave in early rounds.', ['team', 'role|day', 'success|expectation', 'learning|mentor|training', 'tech|stack'], 'medium'],
    ['How do you show confidence through body language?', 'Sit upright with relaxed shoulders, maintain natural eye contact, keep hands visible and calm, nod while listening, speak at a steady pace and smile when appropriate. Avoid crossing arms, fidgeting or looking at the floor.', ['posture|upright|sit', 'eye contact', 'hands|gesture', 'smile', 'pace|voice'], 'easy'],
  ],
};

